Content-Type: text/plain;charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable



On Thu, Apr 4, 2019, at 6:01 AM, Christopher Wood wrote:
>=20
>=20
> On Wed, Apr 3, 2019, at 11:22 PM, John Mattsson wrote:
> > Hi Christopher,
> >=20
> > Thanks for the clarifications.
> >=20
> > >You=E2=80=99re correct that this requires both endpoints to adopt t=
he change=20
> > >simultaneously. However, that does not contradict the quoted text, =
which=20
> > >states that the protocol is not changed.
> >=20
> > I think "this requires both endpoints to adopt the change simultaneo=
usly" is a problem as it makes it impossible to introduce this mechanism=
 in many existing deployments. I would expect a solution that can be int=
roduced gradually (some nodes at a time) in existing deployments support=
ing RFC 8446 and/or RFC 5246.
>=20
> I think perhaps my response was unclear. In your scenario, adding key =
import support is essentially the same as adding a new PSK to a node. So=
me nodes may offer (or accept) the PSK, while others do not. Successful =
use of an imported PSK will only occur if both endpoints have imported t=
he same PSK. Gradual deployment is possible.

To clarify further since this too may be misleading: This assumes the im=
ported PSKs are offered alongside the existing external PSKs. So, to you=
r previous question regarding simultaneous use of imported keys and exte=
rnal PSKs, gradual deployment is only applicable with stacks supporting =
1.3 and 1.2, where imported keys apply to 1.3. Nodes supporting 1.2 and =
1.3 without the imported keys will offer 1.2 PSKs, whereas nodes with im=
ported keys will offer 1.2 and imported 1.3 PSKs.

You=E2=80=99re right that it=E2=80=99s not possible to deploy gradually =
if one is currently using external PSKs for only 1.3 or 1.2, wants to ad=
opt the importer API, and is prohibited from using both imported and ext=
ernal PSKs at the same time.

>=20
> >=20
> > But, if you want to change the behaviour of the using the same exter=
nal PSK in both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 as allowed by RFC 8446, a solution n=
ot requiring "simultaneous change" is probably not possible.
> >=20
> > The requirement that "this requires both endpoints to adopt the chan=
ge simultaneously" should probably be highlighted in the document.
> >=20
> > The current mechanism does not seem to be deployable for e.g. 3GPP u=
ntil TLS 1.4 is introduced, and then only for TLS 1.4 and later... on th=
e other hand, being limited to SHA-256 for PSK may be ok for 3GPP. (and =
3GPP could just derive a Separate SHA-384 PSK if needed).
> >=20
> > Some more comment:
> >=20
> > - Which label should be used by various versions of DTLS?
>=20
> Likely the same labels as the TLS counterparts, since the label more o=
r less specifies the *handshake* variant.
>=20
> >=20
> > - "If a client or server wish to deprecate a hash function and no lo=
nger
> > use it for TLS 1.3, they may remove this hash function from the set
> > of hashes used during while importing keys. This does not affect the=

> > KDF operation used to derive concrete PSKs."
> >=20
> > If someone deprecates the use of a hash function inside TLS 1.3 (use=
d by Transcript-Hash and HKDF), does it then makes sense to keep derivin=
g keys outside of TLS with that same deprecated hash function?
>=20
> No. If this deprecation occurs, then they would follow the advisory te=
xt and not use the old hash when importing keys.
>=20
> >=20
> > - "To mitigate these problems, external PSKs can be bound to a speci=
fic hash function when used in TLS 1.3"
> >=20
> > I would change to "are bound to a specific". If the application does=
 not specify anything, the keys are bound to SHA-256.
>=20
> Works for me. I=E2=80=99ll file an issue to track the change.
>=20
> Thanks,
> Chris=20
>=20
> >=20
> > Cheers,
> > John
> >=20
> > -----Original Message-----
> > From: Christopher Wood <caw@heapingbits.net>
> > Date: Wednesday, 3 April 2019 at 16:25
> > To: John Mattsson <john..mattsson@ericsson.com>
> > Cc: "TLS@ietf.org" <TLS@ietf..org>
> > Subject: Re: [TLS] Comments on draft-wood-tls-external-psk-importer-=
01
> >=20
> > Thanks for the feedback, John. I filed issues on the GitHub draft=20=

> > located at [1]. Please see inline below for comments.
> >=20
> > On 1 Apr 2019, at 12:00, John Mattsson wrote:
> >=20
> > > Hi,
> > >
> > > Thanks for trying to solve this problem! Not having a way to use t=
he=20
> > > same external PSK for different cipher suites is definitely a thin=
g=20
> > > missing from TLS 1.3.
> > >
> > > As I stated during the wg session, 3GPP have a few use cases that =
use=20
> > > PSK-ECDHE between phone and core network. Contrary to what IETF pe=
ople=20
> > > sometimes believe, most 3GPP use of TLS (between phone and core=20=

> > > network, between core nodes, and between different operators) use=20=

> > > mutual authentication with certificates.
> > >
> > > According to the current 3GPP specification, the phone and the cor=
e=20
> > > network would share an external PSK and an external identity. The =
hash=20
> > > function is undefined, i.e. SHA-256 is used for TLS 1.3. The phone=
 and=20
> > > the network would then negotiate TLS 1.2 with any cipher suite or =
TLS=20
> > > 1.3 with SHA-256.
> > >
> > > Some comments
> > >
> > > - The draft should make clear if the External PSK and external=20
> > > identity can be used together with the imported identities. I assu=
me=20
> > > the idea is that they can=E2=80=99t but I can=E2=80=99t see any te=
xt specifying=20
> > > this.
> >=20
> > Indeed, that was the intent.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - =E2=80=9DImported keys do not require negotiation for use, as a =
client and
> > > server will not agree upon identities if not imported correctly.
> > > Thus, importers induce no protocol changes with the exception of
> > > expanding the set of PSK identities sent on the wire.=E2=80=9D
> > >
> > > While I see how the defined mechanism can be deployed in a new=20
> > > deployment, I do not see how to introduce it in an existing=20
> > > deployment. Assuming that the external PSK cannot be used together=
=20
> > > with the imported identities, the TLS client and server must intro=
duce=20
> > > the mechanism at the same time, that is often not possible to cont=
rol.
> >=20
> > You=E2=80=99re correct that this requires both endpoints to adopt th=
e change=20
> > simultaneously. However, that does not contradict the quoted text, w=
hich=20
> > states that the protocol is not changed.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - =E2=80=9Cstruct {
> > > opaque external_identity<1...2^16-1>;
> > > opaque label<0..2^8-1>;
> > > HashAlgorithm hash;
> > > } ImportedIdentity;
> > > =E2=80=9D
> > >
> > > This scales poorly, A client has to send n*m identities where n is=
 the=20
> > > number of supported versions and m is the number of supported hash=
=20
> > > algorithms...
> >=20
> > Yes, this is a tradeoff we made by opting to not modify the key=20
> > schedule.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - Would it not be possible to just use the version number as label=
?=20
> > > I.e. 0x0304 instead of =E2=80=9Ctls13=E2=80=9D. In that way you do=
 at least not=20
> > > have to define a new labels.
> >=20
> > We probably could, though since the labels are subject to change (se=
e=20
> > the TODO about possibly including the HashAlgorithm in the label), w=
e=20
> > kept it as is.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - =E2=80=9CTLS 1.3 [RFC8446] supports pre-shared key (PSK) resumpt=
ion,=20
> > > wherein
> > > PSKs can be established via session tickets from prior connections=
 or
> > > externally via some out-of-band mechanism.=E2=80=9D
> > >
> > > This seems to indicate that all use of PSK is resumption which is =
not=20
> > > correct. I suggest changing to =E2=80=9CTLS 1.3 [RFC8446] supports=
=20
> > > pre-shared key (PSK) authentication=E2=80=9D
> >=20
> > Good suggestion! We=E2=80=99ll fix it.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - =E2=80=9Cimporting external PSK (Pre-Shared Key) into TLS.=E2=80=
=9D
> > >
> > > Not sure if =E2=80=9Cinto=E2=80=9D is the right word, I think =E2=80=
=9Cfor=E2=80=9D as in th e=20
> > > title is better. The mechanism seems very much external to TLS its=
elf.=20
> > > I see it as a mechanism to derive several external PSKs from a sin=
gle=20
> > > external PSK.
> >=20
> > Ditto.
> >=20
> > >
> > > - =E2=80=9CSimilarly, TLS 1.2 and all prior TLS versions should us=
e "tls12"=20
> > > =E2=80=9C
> > >
> > > The versions prior to TLS 1.2 will soon be deprecated. And why spe=
nd=20
> > > time updating the TLS 1.2 code base instead of moving to TLS 1.3?
> >=20
> > We=E2=80=99re just covering our bases here. I tend to agree that the=
 focus=20
> > should be on updating to TLS 1.3 instead of =E2=80=9Cpatching=E2=80=9D=
 TLS 1.2=20
> > stacks.
> >=20
> > >
> > >
> > > Suggestion:
> > >
> > > Instead of deriving a large set of new external PSKs, wouldn=E2=80=
=99t it be=20
> > > possible to just use different hash functions in different parts o=
f=20
> > > the key hierarchy? I.e. if SHA-256 is associated with the external=
 PSK=20
> > > and SHA-384 is the hash algorithms in the chosen cipher suite:
> > >
> > > PSK -> HKDF-Extract(SHA-256) =3D Early Secret
> > > |
> > > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "ext binder" | "res binder", "")
> > > | =3D binder_key
> > > |
> > > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "c e traffic", ClientHello)
> > > | =3D client_early_traffic_secret
> > > |
> > > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "e exp master", ClientHello)
> > > | =3D early_exporter_master_secret
> > > v
> > > Derive-Secret(., "derived", "")
> > > |
> > > v
> > > (EC)DHE -> HKDF-Extract(SHA-384) =3D Handshake Secret
> > >
> > > Support of this could be signalled with the tls_flags extension. I=
f=20
> > > the server does not support the extension it must according to RFC=
=20
> > > 8446 chose a cipher suite with SHA-256.
> >=20
> > Modifying the key schedule was discussed in the context of [2].. At =
the=20
> > time, it seemed the desire to not touch the key schedule outweighed =
the=20
> > desire to not inflate the ClientHello.
> >=20
> > Best,
> > Chris
> >=20
> > [1] https://github.com/chris-wood/draft-wood-tls-external-psk-import=
er
> > [2] https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-davidben-tls-universal-psk-00
> >=20
> >=20
> >=20
>=20
> _______________________________________________
> TLS mailing list
> TLS@ietf.org
> https://www.ietf.org/mailman/listinfo/tls
>=20

